we've heard multiple warnings in the  last few days that the NHS is facing  extreme pressure this winter and on  multiple fronts so what do the figures  show one measure of the Health Service  performance is a e waiting times which  have increased dramatically over the  last two years in November 2022 more  than 37 000 people waited more than 12  hours in a e before being admitted to a  hospital Department that's 34 times  higher than it was at the end of 2019.  the latest figures show there's also  been a sharp increase in the number of  people having to wait over an hour for  an ambulance look at the red line that's  2022. on Christmas Day the latest day  for which we have data more than one in  seven people waited more than an hour  for an ambulance that's almost 10 times  higher than in 2021 and 17 times higher  than in 2020 demand for beds is also  higher because there are more long-term  own patients than any of the three  previous years take a look at the third  graph over here it shows over the past  four years the number of beds taken up  by people who've been in hospital for  more than three weeks in the week  leading up to Christmas last year it was  almost 25 percent higher than it was in  2021 and 60 higher than it was in 2020.  so why is demand so high it's at least  partly because of the pressure created  by Rising flu cases let's look at the  flu admissions in the week leading up to  Christmas in 2021 there were just 34  people in hospital with flu each day and  only two of them required critical care  now let's look at a week leading up to  Christmas in 2022 there were  3746 patients each day in hospital with  flu with more than 260 of them in  critical care beds so we're seeing more  than a hundred times more people being  hospitalized with flu and requiring care  and they'll run up to Christmas compared  to the year before and that's all  happening as the Health Service faces a  staffing crisis as of September 2022  there were more than 133  000 job vacancies across the NHS and  around 11 of those vacancies are in  nursing with the nurses Union saying  poor wages and working conditions are  why so many are leaving the sector  now the government says it's doing all  it can to relieve the strains on the  Health Service but with multiple NHS  trusts declaring critical incidents a  winter of industrial action and the  pressure of flu and covert cases it  seems the NHS is in for a challenging  start to the new year
 

